{"id":"20260829T005211.091264684Z-18e5fcf6","time":"2026-08-29T00:52:11.091264684Z","session_id":"session-20260829T003802.236153648Z","task_id":"task-1787963882236621025","source":"claude","kind":"output","summary":"{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01ULmNSjTX4kMntfvXjmYp7T\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"commit fdd5b1d7b9832729caf3c486ccf03eda550f8808\\nAuthor: davecourtois \u003cdave.courtois60@gmail.com\u003e\\nDate:   Fri Aug 28 20:49:58 2026 -0400\\n\\n    fix(workflow): the candidate that is reviewed is the one that is confined -- validation re-reads the diff after a mutating formatter, so the same bound is applied again\\n    \\n    The pre-validation check bounded what the WORKER produced. e.validate then\\n    runs the configured formatters against the candidate worktree and re-reads\\n    the diff, deliberately, because evidence gathered before a rewrite is\\n    evidence about different bytes. Everything downstream consumes that re-read\\n    diff.\\n    \\n    A formatter is a configured command with write access to the whole\\n    worktree. It can create or rewrite a path the plan never named, and that\\n    path then reached the validation evidence, the prospective and test-edit\\n    inspections, the audit, the Level-1 classification, the reviewer and\\n    admission unrefused -- past the only check that reads the plan. Placement\\n    before validation proved placement, not confinement of the candidate\\n    actually reviewed.\\n    \\n    confineToPlannedFiles is therefore called a second time, on the diff\\n    e.validate returns, before ValidationRun is emitted and before any\\n    inspection, audit, classification, review or admission reads it. The\\n    pre-validation check stays: it names the worker as the author of a widening\\n    the worker committed, which a single post-validation check would blame on\\n    the formatter.\\n    \\n    TestAFormatterCannotWidenTheCandidateAfterValidation runs the real path --\\n    a real repository, a worker edit inside the plan that the first check\\n    passes, e.validate with a formatter that writes an unplanned file, and the\\n    diff validation actually returns, which is refused by name.\\n    \\n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e\\n\\n internal/workflow/engine.go      |  14 +++++\\n internal/workflow/engine_test.go | 117 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\\n 2 files changed, 131 insertions(+)\\n=====FULL=====\\ncommit fdd5b1d7b9832729caf3c486ccf03eda550f8808\\nAuthor: davecourtois \u003cdave.courtois60@gmail.com\u003e\\nDate:   Fri Aug 28 20:49:58 2026 -0400\\n\\n    fix(workflow): the candidate that is reviewed is the one that is confined -- validation re-reads the diff after a mutating formatter, so the same bound is applied again\\n    \\n    The pre-validation check bounded what the WORKER produced. e.validate then\\n    runs the configured formatters against the candidate worktree and re-reads\\n    the diff, deliberately, because evidence gathered before a rewrite is\\n    evidence about different bytes. Everything downstream consumes that re-read\\n    diff.\\n    \\n    A formatter is a configured command with write access to the whole\\n    worktree. It can create or rewrite a path the plan never named, and that\\n    path then reached the validation evidence, the prospective and test-edit\\n    inspections, the audit, the Level-1 classification, the reviewer and\\n    admission unrefused -- past the only check that reads the plan. Placement\\n    before validation proved placement, not confinement of the candidate\\n    actually reviewed.\\n    \\n    confineToPlannedFiles is therefore called a second time, on the diff\\n    e.validate returns, before ValidationRun is emitted and before any\\n    inspection, audit, classification, review or admission reads it. The\\n    pre-validation check stays: it names the worker as the author of a wid